WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
- Under the streets of Vancouver,

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.240
at the University of British Columbia,

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.300
there are pneumatic tubes
2½ kilometers long

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.798
that use air pressure to transport
slightly radioactive stuff

00:00:10.798 --> 00:00:13.170
underground from a particle accelerator
to a hospital.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.420
The tubes are called the Rabbit Line

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.160
and the only sign of
them from the surface

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.130
is a few manholes, and some
little studs in the ground

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:21.270
to mark the path.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.910
Most of the students and staff
here at UBC have no idea

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.220
that a few times every day,
underneath their feet,

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:31.170
capsules with radioactive stuff speed by
at up to 100 kilometers an hour.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.420
So, we're going to start at
the particle accelerator here

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.360
and explain how: how the
stuff's made and how it's sent.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.610
And then, at the receiving end,
at the hospital,

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.380
we're going to explain why.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.080
- This is the GMP Lab at TRIUMF,
and here,

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.252
we use medical isotopes
and make tracers

00:00:46.252 --> 00:00:48.750
for imaging and therapy.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.600
So, in accelerators,
we have cyclotrons.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.490
So cyclotron: we inject H-, so
negative charges of hydrogen,

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.920
and they spin around and get
really, really fast.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.390
Carry a lot of energy.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:03.750
And then we extract
that H- to proton.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:06.240
And then we use a proton beam
to hit a target.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.270
Once they hit a target, it will
produce radioactive isotopes.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:10.770
And once we have the isotopes,

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.690
they will be transferred here.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.390
And here, our chemists
will do their magic.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:19.680
So they combine with some
injectable liquid, mostly saline.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.280
The ones processed in this
lab are short-lived isotopes,

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.370
two in particular, one is carbon-11.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:29.310
And the carbon-11 only
has 20 minutes half-life.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.860
Our chemists doing a very, very fast job,

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.110
and then they send this
through the Rabbit Line

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.900
to UBC Hospital and inject patients.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.580
For the long-lived isotopes,

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.890
we produce probably nanogram range.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:44.130
The short-lived one is even
smaller than that amount.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:45.120
That's all you need.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.790
- So, particle accelerator
upstairs makes the isotopes.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.070
Chemists in the clean room
turn them into a liquid.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.470
The team in this room put
that vial inside a rabbit,

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.480
a capsule, and into that tube.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:55.620
There's one already loaded.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:56.910
Close it up, push the right buttons,

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:59.250
and 100 psi of air pressure accelerates it

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.440
to a top speed of 100 kilometers an hour.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.540
Now, I am not allowed to
send a camera down the line.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.190
It's really carefully calibrated.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.920
You don't take risks like
that with medical treatments.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:08.850
Plus, it's pitch black.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.530
I'd also have to send a light as well.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.470
But I did ask if I could push the button.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.820
It's just the big one
marked send, right? Okay.

00:02:16.823 --> 00:02:21.823
[machine beeping]
[air whooshing]

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.723
That is surprisingly loud.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.395
- Because we are sending
it over underground, 

00:02:30.395 --> 00:02:33.480
soil under the road will serve
as a very good shielding,

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.150
also because the amount we
send is very, very little,

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.310
we're sending a dose that's actually safe

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:41.190
to inject in a human inside a body.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.020
So for the public, there's no hazard.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.180
If we drive up the street,

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.700
it would take about 10, 15 minutes.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.340
But if we shoot through the Rabbit Line,

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.170
it only takes a little
bit over two minutes.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.730
And it's more reliable.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.890
And we don't need to stop at stop signs!

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.210
- About 2½ minutes later,

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:00.120
the capsule arrives here
at the University Hospital.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.290
But that does raise the question,

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.600
when the capsule is going that fast,

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.810
how does it stop in the
right place every time?

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.040
- When it's close to the end,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.380
about 10 meters to the end,
there is a vent.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.750
So you will lose the pressure
from the compressed air.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.840
And also, the front,
the air is compressed,

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.930
so it serve as a buffer to
slow down the rabbit as well.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:21.631
And that which allows the rabbit
to drop safely at hospital.

00:03:21.631 --> 00:03:22.890
[capsule thumping]

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:24.390
- That isn't the same capsule.

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:25.560
It's a 10-minute drive away.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.300
The Rabbit Line is incredibly fast.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.640
Which leaves the big question, why?

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.430
Well, what's being sent
are radiopharmaceuticals,

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:35.100
slightly radioactive stuff
that here is meant

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.020
for research in medicine.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.060
Obviously, I'm not filming
any research subjects here,

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:40.650
but the staff let me know
what sort of thing

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.000
this can be used for.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.820
- When this radioactive substance decays,

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.940
it emits gamma rays, which we detect.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:52.230
In doing that, we can then
reconstruct the image.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.420
You can actually see how the brain works

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:56.880
without really getting into the brain.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.870
A very, very important picture
that you couldn't get otherwise.

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:05.640
By definition, they cannot have any
 pharmacological effect on the body.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.580
If we were to inject anything
that changes the system,

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.170
we're not looking at the system.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:12.570
We're looking at the system as modified.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:13.760
In Parkinson's disease,

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.500
by the time you have clinical symptoms,

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.680
the brain is already very, very damaged.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.260
That's why we observe populations at risk.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:24.240
And then, of course,
we've done some studies

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.240
in psychiatry, mood disorders,
in dementia, addiction.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:34.320
The discovery that there is a
neurochemical placebo effect,

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.910
a neurotransmitter release
when people were expecting

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:40.710
some benefit of a drug,

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.290
even when a drug was not given.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:44.730
- It turns out, around the world,

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:47.340
quite a few hospitals have
their own particle accelerators,

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.050
maybe for diagnosis, maybe for treatment,

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:50.550
maybe like here, for research.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:52.680
Although they do normally use
a slightly less dramatic way

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.030
of transporting the results.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.340
Which left me with two questions.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:57.510
No one could answer the first,

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:59.490
which is why it's called the Rabbit Line.

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.020
Apparently, no one here knows.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:02.520
But the second one was,

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.580
how do they get the
rabbits back to the start?

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.100
- Drive it, we pick it
up or they send it over.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.683
Old-school! [laughing]

